  {
    "insert": "The goal of the research is to improve data privacy in LLM education tools. The education tools should fulfill legal requirements, like data minimization, purpose limitation and transparency, and handle the student's data in an ethical manner. To achieve this, the framework Privacy by Design (PbD) is used to integrate data privacy protection into the development of LLM education Tools at every stage of system design.\n\nYou have a great hook, that immediately caught my attention regarding the problem. The SOTA section and the explanation of ethical and legal questions regarding data privacy are very informative. The explanation to your approach is easy to follow and shows your intention clearly. The theoretical framework explanation is very thorough and highlights the pros of PbD well. The phrases are divided wisely by taking the issue prediction and limitations of the approach into account.\n\nYour exposé is in general very well structured. However, there are some aspects I'd like to highlight.\nFirstly, the motivation should emphasize the necessity and urgency of integrating privacy protection into LLM-tools. In your exposé, your anker is missing some explanation of key details, like why the personal data of students is necessary for the education tools. The explanation should contain a section of what type of personal data is used in the LLM tools and why it is needed. Additionally, you mainly highlighted the necessity to integrate data privacy but not the consequences of not having them. I would suggest adding a short discussion of the possible consequences, on the one hand the students that might be affected by a possible breach, on the other hand the possible legal implications of a data leak.\n\nSecondly, answering the sub-research questions should be helpful to answer the main research question. In your research, you listed the ethical challenges but not the practical ones. For that, I think you should add some practical challenges in embedding privacy preserving design principles into the development of LLM-tools. Furthermore, the details on how you collect the challenges and the methods to overcome these challenges should also be added to answer the sub-questions.\n\nLastly, the goals of citations are to indicate to the reader, which research literature is referring to. The introduction contains some general assertions without citations (\"For instance, some studies indicate...\", \"So far, the issue has primarily been addressed...\"). I recommend you adding the citations of the referring paper to the end of sentence, so that you only have one citation style."
